1. WHAT THIS LICENCE IS

Atlas is proprietary, closed-source software owned by Mohammed Mustafa
Hussain ("the Licensor"). It is not open-source software and it is not
publicly distributed.

This licence gives a specifically invited tester permission to use Atlas free
of charge during a controlled beta, for evaluation and testing. It gives no
other permission.

If the Licensor did not invite you, you have no licence to the Atlas-Owned
Material. If you do not accept these terms, do not install or use Atlas.


2. DEFINITIONS

"Atlas" means the FounderOS Atlas software the Licensor provides to you.

"You" means a person the Licensor has specifically invited to the controlled
beta, and the organisation you represent.

"The controlled beta" means the evaluation period that begins when the
Licensor gives you access to Atlas and ends when the Licensor tells you it
has ended, or when this licence ends under section 15, whichever happens
first.

"Atlas-Owned Material" means the parts of Atlas the Licensor owns: its source
code, compiled code, templates, documentation and design. It does not include
Third-Party Components.

"Third-Party Components" means software included with or used by Atlas that
others own and license under their own terms. THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt
identifies them.

"Your Data" means information you give Atlas, or that Atlas collects from
systems you run it against — including network configurations, device
information, credentials, and any customer information you import.

"Your Outputs" means what Atlas produces from Your Data: reports, topology
diagrams, exports, findings, analyses, compliance results,
configuration-derived reports and diagnostics — whether they describe your own
environment or a network you run Atlas against for someone else, and even
where Atlas builds its own templates or viewer code into them.


3. THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS COME FIRST

Atlas includes Third-Party Components. Each one stays under its own licence.

Nothing in this licence removes, reduces or overrides any right those licences
give you — including any right to copy or redistribute a component, to modify
it, to reverse engineer it, or to receive its source code.

Where those licences and this licence disagree about a Third-Party Component,
those licences win.

THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt accompanies Atlas. It identifies the third-party
components, states the licence for each one for which licence evidence is
held, and reproduces the licence texts. Some components are licensed under the
GNU Lesser General Public License version 2.1 or the Mozilla Public License
2.0, which entitle you to their source code; that source is supplied with
Atlas, and where it is not, the Licensor will give it to you on request at no
more than the cost of supplying it. Read THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt alongside
this licence.


4. YOUR BETA LICENCE

The Licensor grants you a personal, non-exclusive, non-transferable, revocable
licence to install Atlas, to copy it for your own use, and to use it during
the controlled beta for evaluation and testing.

This licence is free of charge. It creates no subscription and no commercial
licence, and it puts the Licensor under no obligation to continue the beta, to
provide support, or to release any future version.

People within your organisation may use your installation of Atlas for your
evaluation and testing, and you are responsible for their use of it. You may
not transfer this licence, and you may not give access to the Atlas-Owned
Material to anyone outside your organisation whom the Licensor has not
invited.


5. BETA STATUS AND EVALUATION USE

Atlas is pre-release beta software. This licence permits evaluation and
testing. It is not a production-use licence, and the Licensor does not
authorise you to rely on Atlas as the basis for critical operational
decisions.

That is a limit on the permission you are given, not a technical restriction.
Atlas contains no expiry timer, no kill switch, no licence key, no activation,
no machine binding and no phone-home mechanism, and this licence introduces
none.


6. WHAT YOU MAY NOT DO WITH ATLAS-OWNED MATERIAL

Except as sections 3, 7 and 9 expressly permit, you may not:

  (a) redistribute Atlas-Owned Material, or give a copy of it to anyone
      outside your organisation whom the Licensor has not invited;
  (b) sell, rent, lease or sublicense Atlas-Owned Material;
  (c) host Atlas-Owned Material as a service for third parties, or operate it
      as a service bureau. Running Atlas yourself during an engagement and
      giving that client Your Outputs is not hosting Atlas as a service;
  (d) modify Atlas-Owned Material;
  (e) remove or alter copyright, licence or attribution notices in the
      Atlas-Owned Material, or the notice files supplied with Atlas.

These restrictions apply to Atlas-Owned Material only. They do not apply to
Third-Party Components, to Your Data, or to Your Outputs.


7. PERMISSIONS REQUIRED BY THIRD-PARTY LICENCES

Some Third-Party Components supplied with Atlas are licensed under the GNU
Lesser General Public License version 2.1, which THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt
identifies and reproduces. That licence allows Atlas to reach you under the
Licensor's own terms only if those terms permit you to modify the combined
work for your own use and to reverse engineer it in order to debug those
modifications.

Accordingly, and despite sections 6(d), 6(e) and 8, you may:

  (a) modify Atlas — including the Atlas-Owned Material — for your own use;
      and
  (b) reverse engineer Atlas so far as necessary to debug modifications you
      make under (a),

to the extent the licence of any Third-Party Component supplied with Atlas
requires that you be permitted to do so. When you do, keep the third-party
copyright and licence notices intact.

This permission is for your own use. It does not permit you to publish,
redistribute, sublicense or sell the Atlas-Owned Material, modified or not,
and section 6 continues to apply to those activities.

This permission is not revocable for a copy of Atlas already supplied to you.
If this licence ends, you may keep and continue to modify for your own use a
copy you had already modified under this section.

Modifying Atlas may stop it working correctly, and the Licensor has no
obligation to support a modified copy.


8. REVERSE ENGINEERING

Except as section 3 preserves, as section 7 permits, and as applicable law
allows despite an agreement to the contrary, you may not reverse engineer,
decompile or disassemble the Atlas-Owned Material.


9. YOUR DATA AND YOUR OUTPUTS

Your Data is yours, or the relevant rights-holder's. The Licensor does not own
it and acquires no right to it. Running Atlas over information transfers no
right in that information to the Licensor.

Your Outputs are yours. The Licensor does not own them, acquires no right in
them, and gains no ownership by Atlas having produced them.

You may use, copy, keep, export, publish and share Your Outputs, inside or
outside your organisation, including with clients — subject only to rights you
or others already hold in the underlying information, such as a vendor's
rights in text reproduced from a device.

Where Your Outputs contain Atlas templates, styling or viewer code that Atlas
itself put there, you may share them anyway: the Licensor permits you to
reproduce and share that material as part of Your Outputs.

For the avoidance of doubt, the restrictions in section 6 do not apply to Your
Outputs. Sharing a report Atlas produced about a network you were authorised
to run it against is not redistribution of Atlas.

Atlas runs on your own systems. It does not send Your Data to the Licensor,
and this licence gives the Licensor no permission to collect, receive or
access Your Data, your credentials, or your usage.


10. NETWORKS YOU ARE RESPONSIBLE FOR

You are responsible for having authorisation to access, discover, scan,
collect from and manage every network, device and system you point Atlas at.

This licence lets you use Atlas. It gives you no authority over anyone else's
network, and the Licensor grants none.


11. THIRD-PARTY SERVICES YOU CHOOSE TO CONNECT

Atlas can optionally be configured to send information to an external AI or
cloud service that you choose and configure. This is off by default and
happens only if you turn it on.

If you turn it on, your use of that service is between you and its provider,
under their terms. The Licensor is not a party to it, provides no such
service, and receives nothing through it.


12. FEEDBACK

You keep ownership of everything you create.

If you send the Licensor feedback, suggestions, bug reports or ideas about
Atlas, the Licensor may use them to improve Atlas without payment or
obligation to you.

This applies only to feedback about Atlas. It gives the Licensor no right to
your work, your inventions, Your Data or Your Outputs. Remove Your Data from
anything you send; if something you choose to send does contain Your Data,
sending it gives the Licensor no right to that data and section 9 continues to
apply to it.


13. NAMES AND BRANDING

This licence gives you no rights in the names "FounderOS" or "Atlas", or in
any logo or branding used with them, beyond referring to the software by its
